// #188 (PERF-CLOSURE-NATIVE) reproducer, committed per R15.
//
// Two of the three first-class closure call shapes the ticket's acceptance
// criteria name: a capturing closure called through a VARIABLE, and one called
// through a PARAMETER. (The third, a closure returned from a function, has a
// different blocker and lives in `SYN__first-class-closure-return.shape`.)
//
// Measured at e12c82d2 BEFORE the fix, via
// `shape run --mode jit --native-witness w.json`:
//   * variable  -> `program_fallback: w17-marshal-residual` — the WHOLE program
//                  left the native path. A module-scope `let` creates a module
//                  binding, and calling any module binding was classified as
//                  "a direct call to an imported stdlib function".
//   * parameter -> the program stayed native, but the closure body reported
//                  `installed-not-dispatched` with 0 interpreter dispatches
//                  while running 200 times on the interpreter.
//
// Slice 1 removed the whole-program bail, so both shapes reached the native
// path, but the closure BODY still ran on the interpreter (`__closure_0`:
// `interpreter-fallback`, 0 native / 200 interpreter).
//
// Slice 2 makes the body native in both shapes. `__main__` / `apply` /
// `hot_double` AND `__closure_0` all read `native-dispatched` with zero
// interpreter dispatches:
//   * variable  -> the MIR emitter's guarded direct dispatch
//     (`heap_closure_call_info` + the block-header `function_id` guard in
//     `mir_compiler/terminators.rs`);
//   * parameter -> the trampoline's native closure dispatch in
//     `ffi/control/mod.rs`, which is where a closure the emitter cannot pin
//     statically gets its native call.
//
// This file is the differential anchor: it must keep producing identical
// output under vm and jit, and it must keep reaching the native path at all
// (a re-introduced whole-program bail is invisible to a fixture that already
// bails). The witness facts themselves are asserted in `witness_tripwires.rs`.

fn hot_double(n: int) -> int { return n * 2 }

fn apply(g: (int) => int, n: int) -> int { return g(n) }

let k = 3
let via_variable = |x: int| x * k

let mut a = 0
let mut b = 0
for i in 0..200 {
    a = a + via_variable(i) + hot_double(i)
    b = b + apply(via_variable, i)
}
print(a)
print(b)
